{"subjectId":"javascript","id":"truthy-falsy","title":"Truthy & Falsy Values","group":"Types, Values & Operators","order":7,"html":"<p class=\"lead\">In a boolean context every value is either truthy or falsy — and there are only eight falsy values to memorise.</p>\n<section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-list\"></i>The complete falsy list</h3>\n<div class=\"chips\"><span class=\"chip\"><code>false</code></span><span class=\"chip\"><code>0</code></span><span class=\"chip\"><code>-0</code></span><span class=\"chip\"><code>0n</code></span><span class=\"chip\"><code>\"\"</code> (empty string)</span><span class=\"chip\"><code>null</code></span><span class=\"chip\"><code>undefined</code></span><span class=\"chip\"><code>NaN</code></span></div>\n<p><strong>Everything else is truthy</strong> — including <code>\"0\"</code>, <code>\"false\"</code>, <code>\" \"</code>, <code>[]</code>, <code>{}</code>, <code>function(){}</code>, <code>Infinity</code> and <code>new Boolean(false)</code>.</p>\n<figure data-lang=\"js\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>JavaScript</figcaption><pre><code>import { inspect } from \"node:util\";\nconst values = [0, -0, \"\", null, undefined, NaN, 0n, \"0\", \"false\", \" \", [], {}, -1];\nfor (const v of values) {\n  console.log(inspect(v).padEnd(9), \"→\", Boolean(v));\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>0         → false\n-0        → false\n''        → false\nnull      → false\nundefined → false\nNaN       → false\n0n        → false\n'0'       → true\n'false'   → true\n' '       → true\n[]        → true\n{}        → true\n-1        → true</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-code\"></i>Using truthiness</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"js\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>JavaScript</figcaption><pre><code>const items = [];\nif (items.length) console.log(\"has items\");\nelse console.log(\"empty array (length 0 is falsy)\");\n\nconst name = \"\";\nconsole.log(name || \"Anonymous\");\nconsole.log(!!\"text\", !!0);</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>empty array (length 0 is falsy)\nAnonymous\ntrue false</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"callout callout--warn\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Watch out</div><p><code>[]</code> and <code>{}</code> are truthy. Check emptiness explicitly: <code>arr.length === 0</code>, <code>Object.keys(obj).length === 0</code>.</p></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-arrows-split\"></i><code>||</code> vs <code>??</code> for defaults</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"js\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>JavaScript</figcaption><pre><code>const settings = { volume: 0, title: \"\" };\nconsole.log(settings.volume || 50, settings.volume ?? 50);\nconsole.log(settings.title || \"Untitled\", settings.title ?? \"Untitled\");\nconsole.log(settings.missing ?? \"default\");</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>50 0\nUntitled\ndefault</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"callout callout--note\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Key point</div><p><code>||</code> falls back on <strong>any falsy</strong> value (so a real <code>0</code> or <code>\"\"</code> is lost). <code>??</code> falls back only on <code>null</code>/<code>undefined</code> — usually what you want for defaults.</p></div>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>List the falsy values in JavaScript.</summary><div><p><code>false</code>, <code>0</code>, <code>-0</code>, <code>0n</code>, <code>\"\"</code>, <code>null</code>, <code>undefined</code>, <code>NaN</code>. (Browsers also have the legacy <code>document.all</code>.) Everything else, including empty arrays and objects, is truthy.</p></div></details></section>"}